WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.298
Secure C++ depends on safer abstractions, explicit ownership, validated input, and predictable failure behavior.

2
00:00:05.298 --> 00:00:08.326
The language gives developers many levels of control.

3
00:00:08.326 --> 00:00:18.166
The secure habit is to use the higher-level, clearer option when it fits and reserve low-level handling for places where the contract is documented

4
00:00:18.166 --> 00:00:18.923
and reviewed.

5
00:00:18.923 --> 00:00:25.357
Prefer RAII, standard containers, clear types, and trusted libraries over manual resource and buffer handling when practical.

6
00:00:25.357 --> 00:00:32.926
These tools reduce repetitive cleanup and size bookkeeping, but they still require developers to understand lifetimes, invalidation rules, and error

7
00:00:32.926 --> 00:00:33.305
behavior.

8
00:00:33.305 --> 00:00:39.360
Treat non-owning views, raw pointers, and references as contracts that need lifetime and bounds clarity.

9
00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:45.794
A span or string_view can make a view length visible, but it does not own the storage.

10
00:00:45.794 --> 00:00:52.985
A raw pointer may be fine as a borrowed parameter, but it should not leave readers guessing about ownership.

11
00:00:52.985 --> 00:00:56.769
Validate before parsing deeply, allocating, copying, dispatching, or changing state.

12
00:00:56.769 --> 00:01:02.825
Clear validation rules and explicit failure paths make code easier to test and safer to maintain.

13
00:01:02.825 --> 00:01:08.123
This is especially important at files, networks, IPC, configuration, environment, and user-interface boundaries.

14
00:01:08.123 --> 00:01:12.286
Limit shared mutable state and review synchronization and lifetime across threads.

15
00:01:12.286 --> 00:01:19.098
Then use warnings, sanitizers, static analysis, tests, fuzzing at a high level, and code review as layered support.

16
00:01:19.098 --> 00:01:22.505
Secure C++ is not one feature or one tool.

17
00:01:22.505 --> 00:01:28.560
It is a steady practice of making assumptions explicit and keeping the codebase reviewable over time.
